export const whyStories={
 python:{
  tagline:'Turn curiosity into an experiment.',origin:'Guido van Rossum · CWI, Netherlands',era:'Public release: 1991',role:'Investigate & automate',
  born:'Guido van Rossum began Python at CWI after working on the ABC language. He wanted the convenience of a high-level scripting language with better extensibility and access to operating-system services. Python was first shared publicly in February 1991. Its name came from Monty Python, rather than the snake.',
  timeline:[['Late 1980s','A practical language takes shape','ABC influenced Python’s readable structure, while real systems work motivated a language that could connect to existing software.',0],['1991','Shared with other programmers','Van Rossum posted Python publicly after more than a year of development.',0],['2001','A foundation for the community','The Python Software Foundation was formed to support Python and its intellectual property.',1],['Your starting point','Small scripts become engineering tools','Begin with arithmetic and files. The same habits carry forward into numerical models, experiments, and robotics data.',null]],
  meaning:'That history explains the appeal: Python makes it relatively easy to express an idea and connect existing capabilities. The real advantage for you is the short distance between a question and a result you can inspect.',
  value:[['Useful immediately','Convert units, total a component budget, organize simulation outputs, and plot exported measurements. A tiny program can remove a task you otherwise repeat by hand.'],['Useful during your degree','As the mathematics deepens, reuse functions, arrays, plots, and tests to explore numerical methods and compare models with measurements. The physics still determines whether the answer makes sense.'],['Useful years later','Python can remain your analysis and automation layer even when a robot’s time-sensitive components use C++. Learning it now is not wasted if your specialty changes.']],
  example:{title:'A rover log contains an unexplained temperature rise.',before:'You have hundreds of rows, some missing readings, and no clear picture of when the behavior changed.',work:['Read the CSV and validate timestamps and units.','Plot temperature with gaps visible and compare runs.','Write a repeatable check that flags readings for investigation.'],after:'You turn a vague concern into a reproducible question: which readings changed, when, and under what conditions? A flag is evidence to inspect, not a diagnosis.'},
  tradeoff:'Readable syntax does not protect you from incorrect units, stale notebook state, hidden assumptions, or unsuitable algorithms. Python also does not automatically provide predictable timing for a physical control loop.',
  timing:'Make Python your primary extracurricular language now. Start with ordinary functions, collections, files, and tests before adding specialized packages.',
  proof:'From a blank file, read a short dataset, reject a malformed row, calculate a result, and explain one test that could catch an error.',
  carry:'Decomposing questions, inspecting data, testing assumptions, and communicating results.',lesson:'python-01',project:'python-project-telemetry',
  sources:[['Python FAQ — origins, first release, and name','https://docs.python.org/3/faq/general.html'],['Python history and license','https://docs.python.org/3/license.html']]
 },
 cpp:{
  tagline:'Build systems whose complexity you can control.',origin:'Bjarne Stroustrup · Bell Labs',era:'Development began: 1979',role:'Organize & integrate',
  born:'Bjarne Stroustrup started “C with Classes” in 1979. He wanted the program-organization ideas of Simula alongside C’s efficiency and flexibility for systems work. The C++ name appeared in 1983; the first commercial implementation followed in October 1985. Its first ISO standard arrived in 1998.',
  timeline:[['1979','C with Classes','Work began on a language combining stronger program organization with efficient systems programming.',0],['1983–1985','C++ gets its name and a release','The name appeared in 1983, followed by a commercial implementation and Stroustrup’s first book in 1985.',1],['1998','An international standard','C++98 established the first ISO standard. C++ continued evolving after that milestone.',1],['Your later projects','Components become a system','Use modern containers, clear ownership, and explicit interfaces as a robotics project grows beyond a small script.',null]],
  meaning:'C++ was built around a tension you will encounter in robotics: higher-level structure is valuable, but resources and execution costs still matter. Its history helps explain both its power and its learning curve.',
  value:[['Useful for a growing robot','Represent sensors, messages, states, and components with clear interfaces. Standard containers and resource management help a larger program remain understandable.'],['Useful when timing and resources matter','Learn what allocates memory, how long objects live, and where execution time goes. Measure performance and failure behavior instead of assuming compiled code is automatically fast enough.'],['Useful across specialties','Even if you work mainly in analysis, C++ helps you understand and extend compiled libraries and simulation components. It becomes especially relevant when your work turns toward robotics software.']],
  example:{title:'An inspection robot receives a delayed sensor message.',before:'Each component works alone, but the combined program sometimes acts on information that is no longer current.',work:['Define a message interface with timestamps and explicit units.','Model valid, stale, and missing input as distinct states.','Test transitions and replay recorded messages before connecting hardware.'],after:'The system can explain why it accepted, rejected, or waited for input. You have turned scattered behaviors into a component with a testable contract.'},
  tradeoff:'You must learn compilation, linking, object lifetimes, and a substantial language. Use modern C++ deliberately; manual allocation and advanced templates are not required starting points. C++ alone does not guarantee real-time behavior.',
  timing:'Add it when a robotics or simulation project gives it a purpose. You can learn modern C++ after basic programming without first mastering C.',
  proof:'Build and debug a small component, explain who owns its data, and demonstrate how it handles late or invalid input.',
  carry:'Interface design, ownership, resource lifetimes, debugging, and reasoning about system behavior.',lesson:'cpp-01',
  sources:[['Stroustrup — A History of C++: 1979–1991','https://stroustrup.com/hopl2.pdf'],['Stroustrup FAQ — invention and standardization','https://stroustrup.com/bs_faq.html']]
 },
 matlab:{
  tagline:'Make the mathematics something you can inspect.',origin:'Cleve Moler; later Jack Little and Steve Bangert',era:'Matrix-calculator roots: 1970s',role:'Model & analyze',
  born:'Cleve Moler created the original MATLAB to let students use numerical linear-algebra software without writing Fortran programs. The name means Matrix Laboratory. It began as an interactive matrix calculator. Jack Little and Steve Bangert developed a much expanded version; PC-MATLAB debuted in December 1984.',
  timeline:[['1970s–early 1980s','A teaching problem','Moler wanted students to reach LINPACK and EISPACK routines directly while studying linear algebra and numerical analysis.',0],['1983–1984','A broader engineering tool','A commercial version added important capabilities including functions, toolboxes, and graphics; PC-MATLAB debuted in 1984.',0],['1990','Simulink expands the workflow','Simulink introduced a complementary graphical modeling workflow. MATLAB and Simulink remain distinct tools.',1],['Your engineering classes','Equations meet evidence','Use arrays, solvers, and plots to investigate a model, then check its assumptions and numerical behavior.',null]],
  meaning:'MATLAB’s matrix-centered design comes from its original purpose. It is particularly natural when the problem on your desk is already written in vectors, matrices, and differential equations.',
  value:[['Useful alongside coursework','Translate a classroom equation into a function, inspect several parameter choices, and label a plot clearly. Programming becomes a way to understand the mathematics.'],['Useful for dynamics and thermal models','Compare a simplified model against a known special case and then against measurements. Inspect residuals and numerical error rather than stopping when a solver returns a result.'],['Useful in a model-based workflow','If your projects use MATLAB or Simulink, familiarity lets you work with those tools directly. If you later prefer Python, numerical reasoning and model-verification habits still transfer.']],
  example:{title:'An equipment enclosure cools more slowly than expected.',before:'You have a first-order model, a measured temperature curve, and several plausible time constants.',work:['Implement the model with named quantities and units.','Compare multiple time constants on labeled axes.','Inspect measured-minus-predicted residuals and challenge the assumptions.'],after:'You can distinguish a programming mistake, a numerical issue, and a model that is too simple for the experiment. The graph becomes an argument you can explain.'},
  tradeoff:'The difficult part eventually becomes the mathematics: dimensions, conditioning, solver assumptions, and model validity. MATLAB is commercial, and toolboxes can have separate access requirements. Check institution access before spending money.',
  timing:'Grow MATLAB alongside the courses and experiments that use it. Learn arrays and functions first; add differential-equation and control tools when your mathematics is ready.',
  proof:'Write a clean function, explain matrix versus element-wise operations, and verify one numerical result independently.',
  carry:'Linear algebra in practice, numerical approximation, model validation, and technical visualization.',lesson:'matlab-01',
  sources:[['Cleve Moler — A Brief History of MATLAB','https://www.mathworks.com/company/technical-articles/a-brief-history-of-matlab.html'],['Cleve Moler — The Growth of MATLAB and MathWorks','https://www.mathworks.com/company/technical-articles/the-growth-of-matlab-and-the-mathworks-over-two-decades.html']]
 },
 c:{
  tagline:'Understand what the machine is actually given.',origin:'Dennis Ritchie · Bell Labs',era:'Formative period: 1969–1973',role:'Represent & interface',
  born:'Dennis Ritchie developed C alongside early Unix, drawing on the earlier languages B and BCPL. His history identifies 1972 as the most creative period within its 1969–1973 development. By summer 1973, C was capable enough for the Unix kernel to be rewritten in it. This was a language shaped by systems implementation.',
  timeline:[['1969–1973','From B toward C','Ritchie added a type structure and other changes while building a language suited to the emerging Unix system.',0],['1973','Unix is rewritten in C','The language became strong enough to support a major systems-software implementation.',0],['1978–1989','A book, then a standard','Kernighan and Ritchie’s 1978 book served as a key reference before the ANSI C standard was adopted in 1989.',0],['Your embedded projects','Representation becomes visible','Practice with explicit byte formats, lengths, integer ranges, return codes, and object lifetimes.',null]],
  meaning:'C’s systems origins help explain why so much responsibility remains visible to the programmer. That makes it demanding, but also useful for understanding the boundary between software abstractions and actual storage.',
  value:[['Useful for embedded understanding','Work with the exact representation of a status field or a sensor sample. Learn what can fit in a type, how a buffer is bounded, and how an error is reported.'],['Useful at interfaces','A documented binary packet is a contract between systems. C practice forces you to ask about length, byte order, signedness, and invalid input instead of hiding those questions.'],['Useful even when you use another language','Understanding memory, data layout, and interfaces makes the behavior of libraries and lower-level systems less mysterious. These concepts also clarify why safer abstractions are valuable.']],
  example:{title:'An environmental sensor sends a packet that is too short.',before:'A decoder assumes every message has the expected number of bytes. A truncated packet violates that assumption.',work:['Define the packet’s length, field positions, and byte order.','Check the available length before reading each required field.','Return an explicit error and test empty, short, and valid input.'],after:'The decoder’s behavior is defined for bad data as well as good data. You can explain every byte it reads and the reason each access is allowed.'},
  tradeoff:'A small language is not necessarily easy. C exposes unchecked bounds, lifetime errors, conversions, and undefined behavior. Use warnings, tests, a debugger, and supported sanitizers; one successful run proves very little.',
  timing:'Study C when embedded work, a course, or a systems interface needs it. Keep it separate from modern C++: the two languages share history but have different idioms and rules.',
  proof:'Decode a short artificial packet, reject malformed input, and explain the range and lifetime of every object involved.',
  carry:'Representation, memory discipline, defensive interfaces, portability, and explicit failure handling.',lesson:'c-01',
  sources:[['Dennis Ritchie — The Development of the C Language (author’s paper, CMU mirror)','https://csapp.cs.cmu.edu/public/docs/chistory.html']]
 },
 bash:{
  tagline:'Make useful work repeatable.',origin:'Brian Fox · GNU project; developed further by Chet Ramey',era:'Unix roots → GNU shell → modern workflows',role:'Connect & reproduce',
  born:'Bash was written for the GNU project by Brian Fox and developed further by Chet Ramey. Its name, Bourne-Again SHell, acknowledges Stephen Bourne’s earlier Unix shell. A shell is both a command interpreter and a language for combining commands. Bash carries that Unix tradition into reusable scripts and interactive work.',
  timeline:[['Unix roots','Commands become a workflow','The Bourne shell provided a predecessor; pipelines let one program’s output become another program’s input.',0],['GNU project','Bash reimplements and extends the shell','Fox wrote the original Bash. Its design brought scripting and interactive conveniences together.',0],['1991 onward','Linux is a separate part of the story','The Linux kernel appeared in 1991. GNU tools and Linux combined into complete systems; Bash itself is not the operating system.',1],['Your daily workflow','Save the steps that make a result','Capture commands, inputs, environments, outputs, and failure checks so an experiment is repeatable.',null]],
  meaning:'Bash’s strength is composition: connect tools that already do useful jobs. That is why a small amount of shell knowledge can improve work in every other language without becoming your main programming track.',
  value:[['Useful immediately','Navigate a project, locate a file, inspect a log, and run the same command again with a different input. Learn quoting and paths early so filenames remain intact.'],['Useful for larger experiments','Automate a repeatable sequence of analysis or build commands, record outputs, and stop on a failed step instead of quietly using stale results.'],['Useful across your toolchain','The same command-line habits support compilers, Python environments, version control, simulation tools, and remote Linux work. You spend less energy reconstructing setup steps.']],
  example:{title:'You need to compare 20 simulation runs.',before:'Each run involves repeated commands and manually renamed output files. It is easy to miss one run or mix its results with another.',work:['Define a simple run naming scheme and input list.','Write a small script that quotes paths and checks command results.','Save a run manifest and keep original inputs separate from derived output.'],after:'A future version of you can reproduce the sequence and find which step failed. The automation makes the experiment easier to audit, not just faster to launch.'},
  tradeoff:'Quoting, expansion, exit status, and text streams have rules worth learning carefully. Bash is awkward for complex structured data and numerical models; call a Python program when that is the clearer tool. PowerShell syntax is different.',
  timing:'Learn a small amount alongside Python from the beginning. Build scripts from commands you already understand instead of collecting unexplained one-liners.',
  proof:'Run a harmless batch of analysis commands with filenames containing spaces, preserve the inputs, and report a failed command clearly.',
  carry:'Reproducibility, process boundaries, automation, careful file handling, and understanding your environment.',lesson:'bash-01',
  sources:[['Chet Ramey — The Bourne-Again Shell','https://aosabook.org/en/v1/bash.html'],['GNU project — Overview of the GNU System','https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html']]
 }
};
